Consider magnetohydrodynamic (MHD) turbulence in a plasma that carries a strong mean (DC) magnetic field $\mathbf{B}_0$, such as the solar wind. Let $\delta\mathbf{B}(\mathbf{x},t)$ denote the fluctuating magnetic field, $\delta\mathbf{v}(\mathbf{x},t)$ the fluctuating velocity field, $\delta n(\mathbf{x},t)$ the density fluctuations, and $\delta|\mathbf{B}|(\mathbf{x},t)$ the fluctuations of the magnetic-field magnitude. For each fluctuating field $f$, define the one-dimensional (reduced) power spectrum $E_f(k)$ in the inertial range so that $\langle f^2\rangle = \int E_f(k)\, dk$.

Two classical dimensional predictions for the inertial-range scaling are:
(i) the Kolmogorov scaling $E(k)\propto k^{-5/3}$, derived under the assumption of incompressible, isotropic, hydrodynamic turbulence with a constant energy cascade rate;
(ii) the Iroshnikov–Kraichnan scaling $E(k)\propto k^{-3/2}$, derived for Alfvénic turbulence in which counter-propagating Alfvén-wave packets interact weakly in the presence of a strong mean field $\mathbf{B}_0$.

Observations of solar-wind turbulence routinely report:
(a) magnetic and velocity power spectra with slopes close to $-5/3$ in the MHD inertial range, despite the presence of a strong $\mathbf{B}_0$ that would naively favor $-3/2$;
(b) density power spectra $E_{\delta n}(k)$ and magnetic-magnitude spectra $E_{\delta|\mathbf{B}|}(k)$ that also exhibit slopes near $-5/3$;
(c) a flattening of $E_{\delta n}(k)$ and $E_{\delta|\mathbf{B}|}(k)$ at high frequencies (small scales) approaching the ion-cyclotron / ion-gyroradius dissipation range, while the spectra of vector components of $\delta\mathbf{B}$ and $\delta\mathbf{v}$ instead steepen near the same range.

Provide a first-principles theoretical explanation, derived from the equations of compressible MHD (or an appropriate kinetic extension), that accounts simultaneously for: 
1. Why $E_{\delta\mathbf{B}}(k)$ and $E_{\delta\mathbf{v}}(k)$ display a $-5/3$ rather than a $-3/2$ inertial-range slope in the presence of a strong mean magnetic field;
2. Why the compressive spectra $E_{\delta n}(k)$ and $E_{\delta|\mathbf{B}|}(k)$ also follow a $-5/3$ slope;
3. Why $E_{\delta n}(k)$ and $E_{\delta|\mathbf{B}|}(k)$ flatten near the ion-cyclotron dissipation scale while vector magnetic and velocity component spectra steepen.

